## What are `Span<T>` and `Memory<T>`?

| Feature | `Span<T>` | `Memory<T>` |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| **Type** | `ref struct` – stack‑only, cannot be stored in fields, cannot be captured by lambdas or `async`/`await`. | `struct` – can live on the heap, can be stored in fields, can be used across `await`s. |
| **Underlying memory** | Any contiguous block (array, stack‑alloc, unmanaged memory, a slice of another `Span<T>`). | Any contiguous block *or* a `Span<T>` that has been boxed into a heap‑based wrapper. |
| **Lifetime safety** | The compiler ensures the `Span<T>` never outlives the object it points to. | The compiler can’t enforce that, so you must be careful (e.g., not use a `Memory<T>` that points to a local array after the method returns). |
| **Typical use** | Very fast, zero‑allocation work on short-lived data. | Pass around “references to memory” that can survive across async boundaries. |
| **Conversions** | `Span<T>` → `Memory<T>` via `new Memory<T>(span.ToArray())` or `new Memory<T>(array)` if you already have the array. | `Memory<T>` → `Span<T>` via `memory.Span`. |
| **Copy / slice** | `span.Slice(start, length)` or the new range syntax `span[start..end]`. | `memory.Slice(start, length)` (returns a `Memory<T>`). |
| **Zero‑allocation** | All operations on `Span<T>` are stack‑only and no GC allocation. | `Memory<T>` itself is a tiny struct; accessing the data (via `Span<T>`) is still zero‑allocation. |

> **Bottom line** –  
> • **Use `Span<T>`** when you need speed, have a short‑lived slice, and you’re in a synchronous context.  
> • **Use `Memory<T>`** when you need to hand the slice off to an async method, store it in a field, or otherwise need a reference that lives longer than the stack frame.

---

## Quick Code Walk‑through

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Demo
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // ---------- 1️⃣  Span<T> ---------
        int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };

        // A Span that references the whole array
        Span<int> span = numbers;           // implicit conversion from array

        // Work on the data without allocating anything
        int sum = 0;
        foreach (int n in span)
            sum += n;

        Console.WriteLine($"Sum via Span: {sum}");   // 15

        // Get a slice (sub‑span) – e.g. the middle 3 elements
        Span<int> mid = span[1..4];          // same as span.Slice(1, 3)
        Console.WriteLine($"Middle slice: {string.Join(", ", mid)}");  // 2, 3, 4

        // ---------- 2️⃣  Memory<T> ---------
        // Wrap the array into a Memory<T>
        Memory<int> memory = numbers;        // implicit conversion

        // In an async method we can await without breaking the reference
        int asyncSum = GetSumAsync(memory).Result;
        Console.WriteLine($"Sum via Memory (async): {asyncSum}");    // 15
    }

    // ----- async method that consumes a Memory<int> -----
    static async Task<int> GetSumAsync(Memory<int> mem)
    {
        // Simulate doing something asynchronously
        await Task.Delay(50);

        // We must access the underlying data via .Span
        int total = 0;
        foreach (int v in mem.Span)
            total += v;

        return total;
    }
}
```

### What this code shows

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| `Span<int> span = numbers;` | A `Span<T>` that lives on the stack and points to the array. | No extra allocation; safe as long as the array is alive. |
| `span[1..4]` | Creates a new `Span<T>` that represents a view into the original array. | Zero‑allocation slice; the compiler guarantees safety. |
| `Memory<int> memory = numbers;` | A heap‑based wrapper that can be stored, passed to `async` methods, or captured in lambdas. | Works across `await`. |
| `await GetSumAsync(memory)` | The async method receives a `Memory<T>`; inside we call `mem.Span`. | No boxing/unboxing; only one small struct on the heap. |

